{"id": "6c84b227-d90d-4fd0-9e52-981f9e81a984", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Good, one account to check: `012345678901_1`. Now let me pull the stack's resources, tags, and check for any EKS involvement.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_U3kKAcUbnLEaBIkSNiPVxq", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Gather resource-scoping facts for CloudFormation stack 'orders-web' in eu-west-1, account 012345678901_1, to populate a BCDR/resilience scoping sheet for business owners.",
  "context": "Ops team needs a scoping sheet for the 'orders website' app, backed by CloudFormation stack 'orders-web' in eu-west-1. Need factual, observable data pulled from the account/stack itself -- NOT business judgment calls. Columns to fill from account data: Observed names (from resource tags, e.g. Name, Application, App tags), Accounts (which AWS account id the resources live in), Resources (list of resource types/ids/names in the stack, e.g. ALB, ASG, RDS, EKS cluster, Lambda, etc), Availability tag if present (look for tags like 'Availability', 'Schedule', 'BusinessHours', '24x7'), any Criticality/Tier/Environment tag present on resources, Code label tag (e.g. 'CostCenter', 'AppCode', 'Code'), Parent business application tag (e.g. 'ParentApp', 'BusinessApplication', 'Portfolio'), and EKS namespace IF any resource in the stack is an EKS cluster/workload (check if stack references EKS and look up associated k8s namespace if discoverable).",
  "resources": ["CloudFormation stack: orders-web", "region: eu-west-1", "account: 012345678901_1"],
  "timeframe": "current state (not historical)",
  "criteria": "1) Use CloudFormation describe_stacks to get stack-level tags and outputs. 2) Use list_stack_resources / describe_stack_resources to enumerate all resources in the stack with their logical IDs, resource types, and physical IDs. 3) For key resources (compute: EC2/ASG/ECS/EKS/Lambda, data: RDS/DynamoDB/ElastiCache, networking: ALB/NLB, storage: S3), pull their tags via the relevant describe/list-tags calls to find Name, Application, Availability, Criticality, Environment, CostCenter, ParentApplication style tags. 4) If an EKS cluster is referenced anywhere (stack resources or tags), identify the cluster name and attempt to determine the k8s namespace used by this app's workloads (e.g. via kubectl if an EKS cluster is found and reachable). 5) Note explicitly which of the requested fields have NO data/tags present in the account, since those will need to come from the business owner. 6) Do not guess or fabricate any tag values -- report only what is actually observed in the account, and flag missing fields clearly."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Gather CloudFormation stack orders-web details and resource tags in eu-west-1"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "48700ede-8647-47ce-b757-5b87d4f03eac", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This stack has no EKS, Lambda, or DynamoDB—instead it's an ALB with security groups, an ASG, an RDS Aurora PostgreSQL cluster, EFS, and an S3 bucket. The stack-level tags only include app and team, missing Availability, Criticality, Environment, CostCenter, and ParentApp tags.

I need to pull resource-level tags for the ALB, ASG, RDS cluster/instance, S3 bucket, and EFS using their respective describe-tags or list-tags-for-resource calls, constructing ARNs where needed for RDS cluster and instance resources.

"}], "parent_id": "f34a599a-bf8f-45c6-b021-c66cad888d7c"}

{"id": "9d87174d-f8a0-415b-a517-240cfdf56900", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "There's an EKS cluster "catalog-eks" in the account, but it doesn't appear to be referenced anywhere in the orders-web stack's resources — none of the resource types there are EKS-related, just the ALB, security groups, ASG, RDS cluster, EFS, and related networking pieces.

Checking tags across the stack, everything consistently has app=orders-web and team=platform plus standard CloudFormation default tags, with no custom business tags like criticality, cost center, availability schedule, or parent application anywhere. I hit an access denied error trying to pull EFS tags specifically, so I can't fully confirm those match, though I'd expect consistency with the rest of the resources.

"}], "parent_id": "f34a599a-bf8f-45c6-b021-c66cad888d7c"}

{"id": "d98e46e3-226c-40bc-8663-ee92f44a9eff", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Good data. A couple of BCDR-relevant facts are still missing that I can actually pull from the account rather than ask the business for — Aurora's AZ footprint, DB backup retention, and EFS/S3 backup settings. Let me grab those too before building the sheet.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_nWH2IpDYouBWIMBfVHKLWG", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Pull AZ-resilience and backup configuration facts for the orders-web stack resources in eu-west-1, account 012345678901_1, to support a BCDR scoping sheet.",
  "context": "Already confirmed orders-web stack contains: ALB/Listener/TargetGroup, an Auto Scaling Group (orders-web-asg, 1x t4g.nano EC2 instance, single-AZ eu-west-1a, min=max=desired=1), Aurora PostgreSQL cluster (orders-web-db) with writer instance orders-web-db-1, a DB subnet group (orders-web-dbsubnets-...), an EFS filesystem (fs-0d5e38c95aaf19c28, Name=orders-web-files), and an S3 bucket (orders-web-uploads-012345678901_1). Need factual account-observable data only, not estimates.",
  "resources": ["RDS DB Cluster: orders-web-db", "RDS Instance: orders-web-db-1", "RDS DB Subnet Group: orders-web-dbsubnets-2viwoh9lkk14", "EFS filesystem: fs-0d5e38c95aaf19c28", "S3 bucket: orders-web-uploads-012345678901_1", "ASG: orders-web-asg", "region: eu-west-1", "account: 012345678901_1"],
  "timeframe": "current state",
  "criteria": "1) describe_db_clusters on orders-web-db: report MultiAZ status, number of AZs the DB subnet group spans, BackupRetentionPeriod, PreferredBackupWindow, and whether there are any reader/replica instances (currently believed to be writer-only). 2) describe_db_instances on orders-web-db-1 for its AZ. 3) describe_db_subnet_groups for orders-web-dbsubnets-... to list which AZs it spans. 4) For EFS fs-0d5e38c95aaf19c28: check if it has multiple mount targets across AZs, and whether AWS Backup has a backup plan/vault covering it (list_backup_selections / list_backup_plans, or check PITR/backup policy via efs describe_backup_policy). 5) For S3 bucket orders-web-uploads-012345678901_1: check versioning status and whether it's covered by an AWS Backup plan or lifecycle/replication rule. 6) For the ASG orders-web-asg: confirm which single AZ it's pinned to and whether the launch template/subnets would allow multi-AZ expansion. Report only observed facts, flag anything not found as 'not configured' rather than guessing."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Check Aurora multi-AZ config, RDS backup retention, EFS/S3 backup settings for orders-web"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "c7e7d637-afbd-4c56-9773-38438a9a0cae", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The S3 bucket versioning check came back empty, confirming versioning isn't enabled there. I also see the launch template's VPCZoneIdentifier only references a single subnet in eu-west-1a, so the ASG is currently confined to one AZ and would need that field updated to support multiple subnets for multi-AZ coverage.

Since the DB subnet group already includes subnets across eu-west-1a, b, and c within the same VPC, expansion is feasible -- the VPC has the necessary subnets, it's just the ASG configuration that's limiting it to a single AZ right now. I think I have enough information to put together the report.

"}], "parent_id": "bd72ac76-b2e3-4968-b266-97cb0424badb"}

{"id": "a4be3c74-73a0-4ae3-9cb1-e0f95ed6a323", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Now I have everything needed to assemble the scoping sheet as a single-row table covering orders-web, filling in observed facts and flagging business-input fields clearly, with a note on the single-AZ risk — RTO on AZ loss is total outage until manual intervention. I'll present it as a markdown table plus a notes/gaps section.

Continuing through recovery approach, restore time, EKS namespace, and customer comment columns - all marked TBD since EFS/S3 lack backups entirely and Aurora only has a 1-day retention window, so restore times can't be calculated and the current state is effectively "no failover, no backups." I'll flag upfront that several business policy columns can't be derived from AWS data alone and need confirmation, then move to writing the final response with these resilience risks highlighted.

"}]}
